---
import ToolLayout from "@site/layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { referencesFor } from "@site/lib/references.ts";
import Caveat from "@site/components/Caveat.astro";
import references from "../references.json";
import uk from "../data/targets/uk.json";
import us from "../data/targets/us.json";
import eu from "../data/targets/eu.json";
import tableList from "../data/foods/tables.json";
const { cite } = referencesFor(references as Parameters<typeof referencesFor>[0]);
const standards = [uk, us, eu];

// Everything below about the food tables is worked out from the published files at build time, so it can't drift
// from the data: each table's definitions, its counts, and how many of its foods give a value for each nutrient.
type FoodFile = {
  generated?: string; source?: string; files?: Record<string, string | null>; definitions?: Record<string, string>;
  labels?: Record<string, string>; keys: string[]; nutrients: { key: string; label: string; unit: string }[];
  foods: { common?: boolean; v: (number | null)[] }[];
};
const files = import.meta.glob<FoodFile>("../data/foods/foods*.json", { eager: true, import: "default" });
const tables = tableList.tables.map((t) => ({ ...t, data: files[`../data/foods/${t.basic}`] }));
const single = tables.filter((t) => t.id !== "blend" && t.data);
const nutrientList = single[0].data.nutrients;
const coverage = (t: (typeof single)[number], key: string) => {
  const i = t.data.keys.indexOf(key);
  if (i < 0 || !t.data.foods.length) return 0;
  return Math.round((100 * t.data.foods.filter((f) => typeof f.v[i] === "number").length) / t.data.foods.length);
};
const commonCount = (t: (typeof single)[number]) => t.data.foods.filter((f) => f.common).length;

// Which targets depend on body weight or on energy, read from the target files.
const basisLists = (s: typeof uk) => {
  const out: Record<string, Set<string>> = { perKg: new Set(), pctEnergy: new Set(), perMJ: new Set() };
  for (const p of s.profiles) for (const [k, spec] of Object.entries(p.targets as Record<string, { min?: { basis: string }; max?: { basis: string } }>))
    for (const b of [spec.min, spec.max]) if (b && b.basis in out) out[b.basis].add(k);
  const label = (k: string) => nutrientList.find((n) => n.key === k)?.label ?? k;
  return Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(out).map(([b, v]) => [b, [...v].map(label).join(", ")]));
};
---
<ToolLayout slug="nutrient-planner" page="methods">
  <h1>Methods and sources</h1>
  <p class="lede">How Food Finder and the Planner work, where their numbers come from, what has been checked, and what they can't tell you.</p>

  <h2>In brief</h2>
  <ul class="prose">
    <li><strong>Food values</strong> come from ten national food composition tables, each offered on its own and on an equal footing: the US (USDA SR Legacy) <Fragment set:html={cite("usda-sr")} />, the UK (CoFID) <Fragment set:html={cite("cofid-2021")} />, Germany (BLS 4.0) <Fragment set:html={cite("bls-4")} />, France (Ciqual 2025) <Fragment set:html={cite("ciqual-2025")} />, Denmark (Frida 5.5) <Fragment set:html={cite("frida-55")} />, Finland (Fineli) <Fragment set:html={cite("fineli-20")} />, South Korea (KFCDb 9.3) <Fragment set:html={cite("kfcdb-93")} />, Japan (MEXT, 8th edition) <Fragment set:html={cite("mext-2023")} />, Canada (CNF 2026) <Fragment set:html={cite("cnf-2026")} /> and Australia (AFCD Release 3) <Fragment set:html={cite("afcd-3")} />. Each value is the table's own, as published, with the table's own label where it says a value is estimated, calculated from a recipe, borrowed, a trace or below a limit.</li>
    <li><strong>The Blended table</strong>, an alpha (an early version), is used until you pick another table: foods found to be the same food in at least two independent tables, each value the median of those tables. The matching is automated, without manual human verification.</li>
    <li><strong>Targets</strong> come from the official UK, US or EU recommended intakes for your age and sex; safety limits are the US tolerable upper intake levels in every standard.</li>
    <li><strong>Food Finder</strong> shows one food, in the amount you choose, against the same targets, charts and notes as the Planner.</li>
    <li><strong>Suggestions</strong> (Planner) are found by an optimiser running in your browser that gets as close as it can to every target without going over an upper limit or outside your calorie range.</li>
    <li><strong>Missing data stays missing</strong>: it is shown as NA (no data), never counted as zero.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="small muted">Open a section below for the details. What has been checked, the limitations and the changes are at the end.</p>

  <details>
    <summary>Inputs and defaults <span class="summary-note">every option and where it starts</span></summary>
    <div class="details-body">
      <p>Both pages start from the same defaults. A setting is kept in the page address, so a link reproduces the result.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Range and notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Sex</td><td>Female</td><td>Female or male; chooses the row of each standard's tables.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Age</td><td>35</td><td>1 to 110 whole years; chooses the age band, or the exact year where the source gives one (for example EFSA protein and energy for children).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Body weight</td><td>EFSA reference body weight for your age and sex <Fragment set:html={cite("efsa-2017")} /></td><td>10 to 300 kg. Used only for targets set per kilogram (listed under "How targets are worked out"). Reference weights (EFSA 2017, Table 17): 18 and over, 68.1 kg male and 58.5 kg female; children by band (1–3: 12.2 / 11.5; 4–6: 19.2 / 18.7; 7–10: 29.0 / 28.4; 11–14: 44.0 / 45.1; 15–17: 64.1 / 56.4). Used for every standard, so the three standards are compared for the same person.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Standard</td><td>UK</td><td>UK, US or EU recommendations (see "Recommended intakes").</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pregnancy or breastfeeding</td><td>Neither</td><td>Uses the source's values for that life stage, and leaves out the foods the NHS says to avoid then.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Diet</td><td>No restriction</td><td>Vegetarian hides meat and fish; vegan also hides dairy, eggs and honey.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Calorie range</td><td>The standard's energy estimate for your age and sex, ±10%</td><td>300 to 6,000 kcal. UK: PHE estimated average requirement <Fragment set:html={cite("phe-2016")} />; US: the <em>Dietary Guidelines</em> sedentary estimate <Fragment set:html={cite("dga-2020")} />; EU: EFSA average requirement at the lowest activity level its table gives (sedentary for adults and under-10s, moderately active for 10–17) <Fragment set:html={cite("efsa-2017")} />. These are low-activity estimates; raise the range if you are more active.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Food table</td><td>Blended, an alpha</td><td>Any of the ten tables, or Blended.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Food list (Planner)</td><td>Common foods</td><td>Common foods or All foods; any food can be switched on or off by hand.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ingredients (Planner)</td><td>Up to 6</td><td>1 to 15 foods per suggestion.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Options (Planner)</td><td>3</td><td>1 to 50 different suggestions.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Vitamin D from food (Planner)</td><td>Off</td><td>When off, suggestions don't try to reach the vitamin D target (see "Vitamin D and oily fish").</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Other standards on the charts</td><td>On</td><td>Draws the other two standards' targets for the same person as thin outlines.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Your own targets</td><td>None</td><td>A target you type replaces the standard's, as a fixed amount.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Amount (Food Finder)</td><td>One unit (an egg, a slice, a banana) or 100 g</td><td>Can be changed.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Food composition data <span class="summary-note">the ten tables, their definitions, and what each gives</span></summary>
    <div class="details-body">
      <details>
        <summary>The tables used</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
          <p>Each table is converted from an archived copy of its published file, listed with its checksum in the references. "Planner list" is its basic foods (see "Which foods are included"); "Common" is how many of those are common foods.</p>
          <div class="table-scroll"><table>
            <thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>Country</th><th>File used</th><th>Licence</th><th>Foods</th><th>Planner list</th><th>Common</th><th>Built</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {single.map((t) => (
                <tr><td>{t.title}</td><td>{t.country}</td><td><code>{Object.values(t.data.files ?? {}).filter(Boolean).join(", ")}</code></td><td>{t.licence}</td><td>{t.foods.toLocaleString("en-GB")}</td><td>{t.basicFoods.toLocaleString("en-GB")}</td><td>{commonCount(t).toLocaleString("en-GB")}</td><td>{t.generated}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          {tables.filter((t) => t.notice).map((t) => <p class="small muted">{t.title}: {t.notice} (<a href={t.noticeUrl}>licence</a>).</p>)}
          <p class="small">The tables are not refreshed automatically: their formats change between releases, so a new release is checked by hand before it is used.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How each table defines its nutrients</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
          <p>
            Tables measure some nutrients differently: energy uses different conversion factors, vitamin A is given as
            retinol equivalents or retinol activity equivalents, folate as total folate or dietary folate equivalents, and
            carbohydrate as available carbohydrate or "by difference". USDA's definitions are those of the SR Legacy
            documentation <Fragment set:html={cite("usda-sr-doc")} />. Where another table's definition differs, it is listed
            below, as recorded in the table's file, and shown with its values as an assumption.
          </p>
          {single.filter((t) => t.data.definitions && Object.keys(t.data.definitions).length).map((t) => (
            <details>
              <summary>{t.title}</summary>
              <div class="details-body">
                <ul class="prose small">
                  {Object.entries(t.data.definitions!).map(([k, v]) => <li><strong>{nutrientList.find((n) => n.key === k)?.label ?? k}:</strong> {v}</li>)}
                </ul>
                {t.data.labels && Object.keys(t.data.labels).length > 0 && <>
                  <p class="small">Labels this table puts on values, kept with each value:</p>
                  <ul class="prose small muted">{Object.entries(t.data.labels).map(([, v]) => <li>{v}</li>)}</ul>
                </>}
              </div>
            </details>
          ))}
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Values worked out by this site</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
          <p>Only these values are calculated rather than read from a table, and each only when every part is known (a missing part is never taken as zero):</p>
          <ul class="prose">
            <li><strong>Carbohydrate (USDA, CNF and KFCDb).</strong> These tables give "carbohydrate by difference", which includes fibre <Fragment set:html={cite("usda-sr-doc")} />. Targets and food labels count carbohydrate without fibre, so the food's fibre is subtracted (not below 0). A food with no fibre value keeps the table's figure. The other tables give carbohydrate that already excludes fibre: Frida as carbohydrate by difference minus fibre <Fragment set:html={cite("frida-55")} />, the rest as available carbohydrate.</li>
            <li><strong>Salt</strong> = sodium (mg) × 2.5 ÷ 1,000, in grams, as on food labels <Fragment set:html={cite("eu-1169")} />.</li>
            <li><strong>Niacin equivalents</strong> = preformed niacin (mg) + tryptophan (g) × 1,000 ÷ 60, where the table doesn't give niacin equivalents itself.</li>
            <li><strong>Methionine + cysteine</strong> and <strong>phenylalanine + tyrosine</strong>: the sum of the two, as the WHO amino acid requirements are set <Fragment set:html={cite("who-2007")} />.</li>
            <li><strong>EPA + DHA</strong> = (EPA + DHA, g) × 1,000, in mg.</li>
            <li><strong>Energy (AFCD)</strong>: kcal = kJ ÷ 4.184.</li>
            <li><strong>Energy (BLS, 411 foods with oligosaccharides)</strong>: the published kcal minus 2 kcal per gram of oligosaccharides, which the BLS 4.0 errata say were counted twice <Fragment set:html={cite("bls-4")} />. For example, glucose syrup: 413 kcal published, 318 kcal used (79.5 g carbohydrate × 4).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which foods are included</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          Food Finder lists every food of every table, with its table in brackets, for example "(USDA)" or "(CoFID)". The
          Planner lists each table's <strong>basic foods</strong>, because it suggests foods: no dishes, cakes, sweets,
          drinks (except plant drinks), fried, coated or salted foods, offal or raw meat, and no values weighed with
          inedible parts. Its default list, <strong>Common foods</strong>, holds the basic foods found as the same food in
          at least two independent tables, leaving out raw foods people don't normally eat raw. Two records count as the
          same food when their names describe the same food and preparation (after synonyms) and their protein, fat and
          energy agree within set tolerances. A fortified product isn't the plain food: two milks whose vitamin D differs
          more than 0.5 µg and three-fold, or two plant drinks whose added nutrients differ the same way, or a name that
          states a vitamin D dose and one that doesn't, are kept apart; so are milk products whose sugars differ by more
          than 4 g and half (fruit or flavoured yoghurt), and "flavoured" foods from plain ones. Records whose values a table says come from another table, or that are
          nearly identical to another table's values (of 8 or more values both give, 70% within 2%; or their protein, fat,
          minerals and water-soluble vitamins within 1%), don't count as independent: for example, most Canadian (CNF) values are taken from the USDA table. The matching is automated,
          without manual human verification, and is a beta; it chooses foods only, and the values are always the
          table's own. <strong>All foods</strong> switches on every basic food. US products with added nutrients (enriched
          or fortified) are marked "US-fortified" and aren't included in All foods, because the UK versions may differ.
          The rules, the list of matches and a report live in the repository's <code>tools/nutrient-planner/</code>
          folder so anyone can check them.
        </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>The Blended table</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
          <Caveat kind="alpha">An early version: the matching is automated, without manual human verification, and errors found in the food tables are still being corrected.</Caveat>
          <p>
            The Blended table holds the common foods found to be the same food in at least two independent tables,
            under stricter rules than the Common list: the same food words (only origin or farming may differ), the same
            preparation, packing, skin and salt, no fortified products or mixtures, colour or variety must not differ,
            and the composition must agree: protein within 1 g or 20%, fat within 0.4 g or 10%, fibre within 4 g or 55%,
            energy within 30%, and at least three of sodium, calcium, potassium, iron and magnesium compared, with at
            most one outside its tolerance. Every two records in a group must pass (not just each record with one other).
            For USDA and CoFID, the earlier reviewed decisions are kept. Pairs found wrong in the reviews are listed, with
            the reason, so they are never grouped.
          </p>
          <p>
            Values are combined only within one definition. For each nutrient, the definition your standard's targets
            use is chosen when any table gives it (for example retinol equivalents for UK and EU vitamin A, retinol
            activity equivalents for US), otherwise the most common one, marked as an assumption. Each value is the
            median of the independent tables that give it (with two tables, their mean), with the range, each table's
            value and any caveats shown. Values a table took from another table, or nearly identical ones, count once. A
            value only one table gives fills the gap and is marked. Values more than two-fold apart are flagged; where
            only two tables give a value and they are more than five-fold apart, no value is given. Plant foods are
            given no EPA, DHA or retinol. For a day with blended foods, each nutrient also shows the lowest and highest
            possible total (every blended food at its lowest or highest value): these are bounds, not a likely error,
            and the result uses the median.
          </p>
          <p class="small muted">Blended: {tables.find((t) => t.id === "blend")?.foods} foods, built {tables.find((t) => t.id === "blend")?.generated}. The rules and two independent reviews are in <code>tools/nutrient-planner/pipeline/blend/</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How many foods give each nutrient</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
          <p>The share of each table's Planner list that gives a value for each nutrient. A low share means most foods show NA for it, and a day's total for it can only be "at least" the known part.</p>
          <div class="table-scroll"><table class="small">
            <thead><tr><th>Nutrient</th>{single.map((t) => <th>{t.tag}</th>)}</tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {nutrientList.map((n) => (
                <tr><td>{n.label} ({n.unit})</td>{single.map((t) => <td>{coverage(t, n.key)}%</td>)}</tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Recommended intakes <span class="summary-note">UK, US and EU targets, how they are worked out, and how they were checked</span></summary>
    <div class="details-body">
      {standards.map((s) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{s.longName}</summary>
          <div class="details-body">
            <p class="small">
              {s.verified?.checked
                ? <><strong>Checked</strong> line by line against the original document ({s.verified.against}) on {s.verified.date}.</>
                : <><strong>Not yet checked</strong> against the original document. {s.verified?.against}.</>}
              {s.verified?.pending && <> <strong>Not yet checked:</strong> {s.verified.pending}</>}
            </p>
            <ul class="prose">
              {s.sources.map((src) => <li>{src.role ? `${src.role}: ` : ""}{src.title} <Fragment set:html={cite(src.id)} /></li>)}
            </ul>
            <ul class="prose muted small">{s.notes.map((n) => <li>{n}</li>)}</ul>
            <p class="small">
              {(() => { const b = basisLists(s); return <>
                <strong>Per kg of body weight:</strong> {b.perKg || "none"}. <strong>As a share of energy:</strong> {b.pctEnergy || "none"}.
                {b.perMJ && <> <strong>Per MJ of energy:</strong> {b.perMJ}.</>} All others are fixed daily amounts.
              </>; })()}
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      ))}
      <details>
        <summary>How targets are worked out</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
          <ul class="prose">
            <li><strong>Choosing the values.</strong> The row for your sex and the age band that contains your age (or the exact year, where the source sets one), and the life-stage row if you chose pregnancy or breastfeeding.</li>
            <li><strong>Per kg of body weight:</strong> amount (g a day) = value (mg per kg) × weight (kg) ÷ 1,000.</li>
            <li><strong>Share of energy:</strong> amount (g) = calories × percentage ÷ 100 ÷ energy per gram, with 9 kcal/g for fat, saturated fat and the fatty acids, 4 kcal/g for carbohydrate and protein, and 3.75 kcal/g for carbohydrate under the UK standard, as PHE converts it <Fragment set:html={cite("phe-2016")} />.</li>
            <li><strong>Per MJ of energy</strong> (EU thiamin and niacin): amount = value × calories × 0.004184 (1 kcal = 0.004184 MJ).</li>
            <li><strong>Which calories.</strong> The targets table and Food Finder show energy-tied targets at the middle of your calorie range. The Planner judges each suggested day against its own calories, which stay inside your range; a day you build is judged against its own calories once it reaches your range, and against the middle of the range until then.</li>
            <li><strong>Upper limits</strong> are the US tolerable upper intake levels <Fragment set:html={cite("nasem-dri")} /> in every standard, because the UK and EU tables used here don't give a complete set; the UK and EU's own limits are used where they give one (for example UK fat and saturated fat, EU sodium <Fragment set:html={cite("efsa-sodium-2019")} />).</li>
            <li><strong>Amino acids</strong> use the WHO safe level of intake per kg of body weight for adults: the average requirement (Table 23) plus 24% (section 8.4) <Fragment set:html={cite("who-2007")} />.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Worked example</strong> (UK, woman aged 35, defaults): energy estimate 2,000 kcal, so the range is
            1,800 to 2,200 kcal and its middle 2,000 kcal. Fat at most 35% of energy: 2,000 × 35 ÷ 100 ÷ 9 = 77.8 g
            (for a suggested day of 1,850 kcal, 71.9 g). Lysine at least 37.2 mg per kg at the reference weight of
            58.5 kg: 37.2 × 58.5 ÷ 1,000 = 2.18 g. Protein at least 45 g (a fixed amount in the UK tables).
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <p>
        The charts can also show the other two standards' targets for the same person (same age, sex, weight and
        calories) as thin coloured outlines, so you can see where the UK, US and EU agree and where they differ.
        The planner always optimises against the one standard you choose.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Food Finder <span class="summary-note">one food at a time</span></summary>
    <div class="details-body">
      <p>
        Food Finder is built from the Planner's own parts: the same food list, targets, charts, notes and references, so a
        change to the Planner shows there too. The differences are that you pick one food, and the details about you
        start from the defaults above until you open "About you" and change them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every food of every table is listed, with its table in brackets, including the Blended table's foods, marked
        "blended, alpha". Amounts start at one unit (an egg, a slice, a banana, weighed as USDA publishes the portion: 50 g,
        32 g and 118 g <Fragment set:html={cite("usda-sr")} />) or 100 g, and can be changed. A food's value for a nutrient
        is its value per 100 g × the amount ÷ 100.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every row is a share of a day's target. Targets set as a share of energy are worked out for the middle of your
        daily calorie range, not for the food's own calories: measured against its own calories, almost any fatty food
        would read as "over the limit".
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>How the planner chooses foods <span class="summary-note">the optimiser, amounts, variety, time limits</span></summary>
    <div class="details-body">
      <details>
        <summary>The optimiser</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          The planner solves a mixed-integer programme with the HiGHS solver <Fragment set:html={cite("highs")} />, running in your browser.
          It chooses at most your chosen number of ingredients and an amount of each, in whole steps, to minimise the total
          <em>relative</em> shortfall across all minimum targets: each target's shortfall divided by the target, so being
          20% short of iron (0.2) counts the same as being 20% short of protein. Upper limits (salt, saturated fat,
          tolerable upper intake levels) and your calorie range are never exceeded: they are constraints, not costs.
          For targets set as a share of energy, the constraint is written against the plan's own calories (nutrient −
          share × energy ≥ 0), and the shortfall is weighted by the target at the middle of the range, because the
          objective must stay linear.
        </p>
        <p>
          A target is planned for only when at least a quarter of the chosen foods give a value for it; otherwise it is
          reported but not planned for. Suggestions are planned from known values only.
        </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Amounts and daily limits per food</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
          <p>Each food has a step (the amounts go up in) and a most per day, by kind of food:</p>
          <ul class="prose small">
            <li>Herbs and spices: 1 g steps, up to 5 g. Oils: 5 g, up to 40 g. Nuts: 5 g, up to 60 g (nut butters 50 g).</li>
            <li>Meat and fish: 10 g, up to 300 g (liver 100 g). Oily fish: 10 g, up to 140 g, shared by all oily fish in the day.</li>
            <li>Dairy: cheese 5 g, up to 100 g; milk and buttermilk 50 g, up to 800 g; yoghurt 10 g, up to 500 g; eggs 10 g, up to 200 g; butter and cream 5 g, up to 40 g; dried, powdered or condensed 5 g, up to 40 g; other dairy 10 g, up to 200 g.</li>
            <li>Plant drinks: 50 g, up to 800 g.</li>
            <li>Fruit: 10 g, up to 400 g; juice 50 g, up to 250 g; dried fruit 5 g, up to 80 g. Vegetables: 10 g, up to 400 g; dried vegetables and seaweed 5 g, up to 30 g. Fruit or vegetable powders: 5 g, up to 30 g.</li>
            <li>Pulses: 10 g, up to 400 g; tofu and tempeh up to 300 g; dry or raw up to 150 g.</li>
            <li>Grains: 10 g, up to 500 g; bread, rolls and similar up to 300 g; dry or uncooked up to 150 g; breadcrumbs, stuffing, croutons and crackers 5 g, up to 60 g. Breakfast cereals: 10 g, up to 100 g.</li>
            <li>Anything else: 10 g, up to 200 g. A few common foods come in units instead: half an egg (50 g each), a slice of bread (32 g), a banana (118 g).</li>
            <li><strong>Children</strong> (under 18): each food's most per day, and the oily fish cap, are scaled by the middle of the calorie range ÷ 2,000 kcal (if that is below 1), rounded down to whole steps and never below one step. This is a rule of thumb, not an official figure.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Variety between options</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          Further options are made to differ: each one shares fewer than half of its foods with any earlier option,
          and no food is used in more than half of the options (at least two). Where that would leave an option
          meeting the targets noticeably less well than the first option (by more than 2% of one target), a food may
          repeat more often, and if that still isn't enough, an option only has to change a third of each earlier
          option's foods. Such an option says so.
        </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Preferences and time limits</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          Among combinations that meet the targets equally well, the planner prefers less food and fewer ingredients
          (tie-breakers of 0.000001 per gram and 0.0001 per food, far smaller than any real shortfall), so suggestions
          tend to sit near the bottom of your calorie range. It also has a mild preference for familiar foods: each food
          from outside the common list adds 0.01 ÷ the number of ingredients, so all of them together cost no more than
          1% of one target, and common foods are chosen unless another food does a noticeably better job.
          Each search is limited to 5 seconds, and stops once it is within 0.1% (or 0.003) of the best possible result.
          If the time limit is reached, the best combination found so far is shown and marked as such; for a long food
          list, the common foods are then searched as well and the better result is shown.
        </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Vitamin D, oily fish and foods to avoid</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          Vitamin D: few foods contain much, so suggestions don't try to reach the vitamin D target unless you tick the
          box for it; the result still shows how much the day provides and the NHS advice on supplements
          <Fragment set:html={cite("nhs-vitd")} />. Oily fish (the fish the NHS lists as oily: herring, pilchards, salmon,
          sardines, sprats, trout, mackerel; not tuna): suggestions include at most one portion (140 g) a day in total,
          and the NHS weekly limits are shown alongside <Fragment set:html={cite("nhs-fish")} />. Liver and liver products
          are left out of suggestions in pregnancy <Fragment set:html={cite("nhs-meat")} />, and shark, swordfish and
          marlin in pregnancy and under 16 <Fragment set:html={cite("nhs-fish")} />; a day you build yourself shows a warning instead.
        </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Missing data, assumptions and caveats <span class="summary-note">NA, "at least", "unclear"</span></summary>
    <div class="details-body">
      <ul class="prose">
        <li><strong>NA, never zero.</strong> Where a food has no value for a nutrient, it is not counted as zero. A day's amount is shown as "NA" (no data) if no food in it has a value, or as "at least" the known total if some do.</li>
        <li><strong>Results.</strong> Met: at least 99.5% of the target. Short: 66% to 99.5%. Low: below 66%. Over: more than the limit (by over 0.1%). When some foods have no value, a result that the missing data could change is "unclear" rather than short or within the limit; a target already met, or a limit already exceeded, by the known values stays so, because missing values can only add to them.</li>
        <li><strong>Codes kept.</strong> Where a table gives a code instead of a number (for example CoFID "Tr", trace, and "N", present but no reliable amount; BLS "&lt;LOD" and "&lt;LOQ", below the limit of detection or quantification), the value is missing and the code is shown with it.</li>
        <li><strong>Zeros.</strong> A zero a table says it assumed rather than measured (BLS "logical zero", CNF "assumed zero", MEXT "(0)", AFCD "imputed zero", Frida "natural zero, not analysed") is kept as 0 and labelled. The AFCD also writes 0 for results below its limit of reporting <Fragment set:html={cite("afcd-3")} />, so any other AFCD zero is missing, labelled "below the limit of reporting". 48 CNF values coded "assumed zero" but given as an amount contradict themselves and are missing.</li>
        <li><strong>BLS errata.</strong> The BLS 4.0 errata (August 2026) apply until BLS 4.1 <Fragment set:html={cite("bls-4")} />: their corrected values are used (skimmed milk retinol 2.4 µg, not 414 µg; low-fat milk iodine 9.5 µg; almond calcium 254 mg; and four more); values the errata say are wrong but not yet corrected are missing (retinol, iodine or calcium of 157 recipes made with those foods; fat, energy and fatty acids of raw anchovy and 16 foods made from it); zero amino acids in foods with protein are missing, because the errata found such zeros to be errors and are reviewing every amino acid.</li>
        <li><strong>Not assessed.</strong> Free sugars (UK limit 5% of energy), because the tables don't separate free from other sugars; any target too few of the chosen foods give a value for (see "The optimiser").</li>
        <li><strong>Caveats shown</strong> with values and results, in one style across the site: Alpha (the Blended table), Beta (the Common list), Assumption (a definition that differs from the one your standard's targets use), Estimate, Borrowed (a value a table took from another), and Translated (Japanese food names, translated by this site from MEXT's English names and a glossary).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Reading the charts <span class="summary-note">scales, colours and symbols</span></summary>
    <div class="details-body">
      <p>
        Each radar chart shows every nutrient as a percentage of its target. The thick navy ring is the target
        (100%). The scale is linear and runs to 150% unless a value is higher, in which case it grows to fit, so
        400% always looks larger than 300%; "Zoom to target" shows the area around 100% instead and marks anything
        beyond the edge with an arrow. Green points meet their goal and red ones don't. "≥" means aim for at least
        the target; "≤" means stay at or below it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The "At a glance" chart follows the nutrition declaration on UK and EU food labels (Regulation 1169/2011) <Fragment set:html={cite("eu-1169")} />:
        energy, fat, saturates, carbohydrate, sugars, fibre, protein and salt. Each is compared with the official
        recommendation for the person where one exists; total sugars, which has no recommendation, is compared with
        the label's adult reference intake (90 g) and shown in grey. Vitamins, minerals and essential amino acids
        (the building blocks of protein) each have their own chart below it, with a table showing which foods
        provide each nutrient.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

