  <p><strong>Not yet checked:</strong></p>
  <ul class="prose">
    <li>Only a sample of each table's values was compared with its file (see above), not every value.</li>
    <li>Fineli: its archived documentation doesn't say how its two fibre values ("fibre, total", used here, and "fibre, total dietary") differ, which forms its vitamin D counts, or how its available carbohydrate is derived. KFCDb: no documentation was archived, so its definitions rest on its column headings.</li>
    <li>BLS recipes whose zinc the errata say will be recalculated (where an ingredient's zinc was missing) are used as published, because the errata don't list them.</li>
    <li>The matching behind the Common list and the Blended table has been checked by automated reviews only, without manual human verification.</li>
    <li>The Japanese food names translated by this site.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="small muted">The full log, with every correction, is in <code>tools/nutrient-planner/VERIFICATION.md</code>.</p>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <p>The results are estimates. Please treat them as a starting point for thinking about your food, not as a precise measurement.</p>
  <ul class="prose">
    <li><strong>Borrowed safety limits.</strong> The UK and EU tables used here don't give a complete set of upper limits, so the US tolerable upper intake levels <Fragment set:html={cite("nasem-dri")} /> are used for safety checks in every standard.</li>
    <li><strong>Other countries' foods.</strong> Each table describes foods as sold where it was made. Fortification differs between countries (for example US milk usually has added vitamin D; UK milk doesn't), and brands differ. Fortified products should be checked against their labels.</li>
    <li><strong>Gaps in the tables.</strong> Some nutrients, such as iodine, vitamin K and choline, are missing from many records in some tables (see "How many foods give each nutrient"), so they are reported as NA or "at least" rather than guessed.</li>
    <li><strong>Different definitions.</strong> A table that defines a nutrient differently from your standard (for example vitamin A or energy factors) is used as published and marked as an assumption; its value can differ from the standard's definition, for example plant vitamin A counted as retinol activity equivalents is up to half the retinol-equivalent figure.</li>
    <li><strong>Absorption isn't modelled yet.</strong> Iron from plants is absorbed less well than iron from meat, ALA omega-3 converts poorly to EPA and DHA, and phytates reduce zinc absorption. These are planned refinements.</li>
    <li><strong>Amino acid targets</strong> apply to adults only. WHO found too little evidence to set separate values for older people <Fragment set:html={cite("who-2007")} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Children.</strong> Targets for children come from the same official tables, but the suggested amounts are adult portion limits scaled down by calories, which is a rule of thumb. The planner is not designed for children under 5.</li>
    <li><strong>Reference values are for healthy populations.</strong> If you have a medical condition, are pregnant, or are planning food for a child, check with a doctor, midwife or registered dietitian.</li>
  </ul>

